-- examples/checks/agg-datastrat-tree.rofl — THE SIZE OF A SUBTREE, a sum over
-- the same relation it concludes: size reads size through its own sum. As
-- relations that is a cycle, and as data it is a tree (a DAG here, e is the
-- child of b and of c), whose depth is the order the sums are sealed in
-- (docs/aggregates.md, "Data-level stratification, as built").
--
--          a
--         / \
--        b   c        size: a 9 (1 + 4 + 4), b 4, c 4, d 1, e 2, f 1, g 1
--       / \ / \       a node counts once for each way it is reached
--      d   e   g
--          |
--          f
edb(node).
edb(child).
node(a). node(b). node(c). node(d). node(e). node(f). node(g).
child(a, b). child(a, c). child(b, d). child(b, e). child(c, e). child(e, f). child(c, g).
size(T, N) :- node(T), S is sum(V ; C : child(T, C), size(C, V)), N is S + 1.
-- how many children have a size, a count of the same component: a leaf has 0, for T is bound from outside
kids(T, K) :- node(T), K is count(C : child(T, C), size(C, _)).
